WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.121
Cargo builds can execute code before the
final application runs.

2
00:00:03.201 --> 00:00:06.440
Build scripts, procedural macros, code
generators, native library discovery,

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.856
and platform-specific compilation steps
may all run on a developer workstation or

4
00:00:09.936 --> 00:00:10.507
CI/CD runner.

5
00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:12.970
That makes build-time behavior part of the
trust boundary,

6
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.622
not a harmless implementation detail.

7
00:00:14.702 --> 00:00:16.894
A build.rs script can inspect environment
variables,

8
00:00:16.974 --> 00:00:19.499
locate native libraries, generate files,
set compiler flags,

9
00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:21.104
or communicate information to Cargo.

10
00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:24.614
Many build scripts are legitimate and
useful, especially for platform integration.

11
00:00:24.694 --> 00:00:27.172
The security point is that they should be
visible, necessary,

12
00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:29.730
and reviewed with the same seriousness as
other trusted code.

13
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.668
Procedural macros and code generators also
affect what gets compiled.

14
00:00:32.748 --> 00:00:35.941
They can create code that reviewers may
not see directly in the source file being

15
00:00:36.021 --> 00:00:36.354
edited.

16
00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:39.055
Teams should understand which macros are
central to their build,

17
00:00:39.135 --> 00:00:41.231
whether generated code is inspectable when
needed,

18
00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:44.837
and whether macro dependencies introduce
additional maintenance or policy concerns.

19
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:46.537
Native dependencies add another layer.

20
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:49.714
They may depend on platform libraries,
link behavior, toolchains, headers,

21
00:00:49.794 --> 00:00:50.604
or system packages.

22
00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:53.114
This can affect portability,
reproducibility, licensing,

23
00:00:53.194 --> 00:00:55.290
vulnerability management, and incident
response.

24
00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.515
A Rust artifact is easier to govern when
native assumptions are documented and

25
00:00:58.595 --> 00:01:00.120
tested in the expected environments.

26
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.678
CI/CD context matters because build-time
code runs where secrets and credentials may

27
00:01:03.758 --> 00:01:04.234
be present.

28
00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:07.650
Runners should not expose unnecessary
registry tokens, deployment credentials,

29
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.017
or sensitive environment values to general
build steps.

30
00:01:10.097 --> 00:01:13.384
Secure Cargo workflows treat build-time
execution as trusted work and design the

31
00:01:13.464 --> 00:01:16.180
pipeline so only the right secrets are
available at the right stage.
